README.md:3:Neon Pairs is a memory game that runs offline from one HTML file, keeps local best moves for each board size and supports shared-device tournaments for four or eight players.
README.md:25:Tournament beside New Game opens setup for four or eight named players. Each knockout match gives both players the same deal in turn; fewer moves advances. Start turn begins each game, Next turn hands over the same deal and Next match advances the bracket. Completed tournaments remain in Past tournaments after reload. Tournament JSON can export or load setup and event progress.
README.md:49:The locked install, type check, Vitest unit tests, build and Chromium journeys passed on the named combination before Tournament mode was added. Those results do not cover the Tournament changes in this version. Other versions have no test record here.
README.md:55:Playwright checks Chromium's accessibility tree through a solo keyboard journey. It checks controls, card names, states, turn results, the final summary, Tab exit and New Game. Tournament mode uses its own status region and bracket tables; no browser accessibility journey for that screen is recorded yet. The developer provides the existing tree evidence and tool record. You confirm spoken output during acceptance with your screen reader.
docs/quality/acceptance.md:13:Finish a game, then start another one during a mismatch. The first journey checks play and completion. The second checks that an earlier timer can't change a new game.
docs/quality/acceptance.md:42:Load an unfinished snapshot, attempt input while paused, then Resume. Check its remaining active time and focus. Load a completed snapshot and check its static banner and unchanged best score.
docs/quality/acceptance.md:44:## Tournament
docs/quality/acceptance.md:46:Run four-player and eight-player tournaments on one device. Check the entry-order pairings, stage board sizes, unique match codes and identical deals for both players in each match. Complete both turns and confirm that fewer moves advances, then exercise equal moves with active-time and seed tie-breaks. Check Start turn, Next turn, Next match, early leave confirmation and the final Champion message.
docs/quality/acceptance.md:48:Inspect every bracket row for both players' moves, time and winner. Confirm a solved board is removed at each handoff, focus moves to the next action and Start turn enters the new board. Reload the page and confirm completed events remain under Past tournaments without changing solo size or best moves. Export setup and active tournament JSON, load each back, and reject malformed, oversized or inconsistent data without replacing the current event.
docs/README.md:18:Choose a size for solo play, then reveal a card and find its partner. Matches stay face up. A miss closes both cards after 800 active milliseconds. Finding every pair completes the game with your moves and time. New Game gives you another deal. Restart repeats the current deal. Tournament beside New Game starts a four- or eight-player knockout event on a shared device. Full details are in [Rules](gameplay/rules.md) and [Competition](competition/competition-overview.md).
docs/README.md:30:A board code describes a deal. A snapshot saves solo progress. A replay records timed actions. Tournament JSON carries event setup or bracket progress. The game algorithms and version 1 formats are in [Reproducibility](sharing/reproducibility.md) and [Data exchange policy](sharing/data-exchange-policy.md). Spec version 2.2 doesn't change the board-code, snapshot or replay versions.
docs/README.md:68:- [new-game-and-restart.md](gameplay/new-game-and-restart.md): What fresh deals, repeats, size changes, loads, Resume, exports, and reloads preserve or reset.
docs/README.md:121:- [data-exchange-policy.md](sharing/data-exchange-policy.md): How board codes, snapshots and replays cross application boundaries, with policy for future exports.
docs/README.md:127:- [format-versioning-guide.md](sharing/format-versioning-guide.md): Format and product versions, exact-key compatibility and changes to artifact meaning.
docs/README.md:144:- [competition-overview.md](competition/competition-overview.md): Purpose, current Tournament mode and its boundary from solo play.
docs/README.md:242:- [changelog-style.md](writing/changelog-style.md): Shows how release notes connect changed behavior with compatibility, source details and verification evidence.
docs/README.md:267:- [release-notes-2-1.md](history/release-notes-2-1.md): Motion review history explaining reduced-motion CSS, completion effects, and unchanged game timing.

