    boardCode: game.boardCode,
    elapsedMs: game.elapsedMs,
    matched: [...game.matched].sort((left, right) => left - right),
    moves: game.moves,
    selection: savedSelection,
    started: game.started,
    version: SNAPSHOT_VERSION,
  };
}

function invalid(message = 'Invalid tournament JSON.') {
  return { kind: 'invalid' as const, message };
}
## Display precision is a separate concern

`formatElapsed` in `src/game/elapsed-time.ts` discards sub-second detail for presentation. It is therefore possible for two visibly identical clock strings to correspond to different `elapsedMs` values. A screenshot of the ordinary status area cannot establish exact equality of active time.

The example with Cora and Dev demonstrates this issue: their stored times differ by 700ms even though a coarser presentation can hide that difference. Reading the rendered text back into a result comparison would throw away information the game already holds.

Snapshot serialization changes precision too, although less dramatically. `encodeSnapshot` floors `elapsedMs` to a whole millisecond. It rounds a pending mismatch's remaining interval upward so that interval remains representable. The game in memory may have fractional time that the snapshot no longer contains. Tournament JSON preserves fractional `elapsedMs` in its embedded current game and match results, while retaining the snapshot rule of rounding a pending mismatch interval upward.

Current in-memory values, exported values and visible text have different precision, so an implementation review needs to identify which representation a prototype actually compares. Treating these as interchangeable would make exact equality depend on an undocumented conversion.

## Pauses and interruptions

The current browser session excludes hidden-page time and time spent waiting for Resume after an unfinished snapshot import. It does not offer an ordinary manual pause control. Sitting with the visible started board while talking still contributes active elapsed time, even when no cards are activated during that conversation.
A snapshot carries `boardCode`, matched position indices, a tagged selection, moves, elapsed milliseconds and the `started` flag, alongside its format version. It does not duplicate the deck. Pair count and completion are derived after loading. Omitting those derived values avoids accepting a record in which the supplied pair total disagrees with the matched positions. [Snapshot format](snapshot-format.md) gives the complete field and validation reference.

A replay carries `boardCode`, an ordered array of `{atMs, index}` actions and `endMs`, alongside its version. It starts from the hidden deal rather than from an imported snapshot. The action timeline advances game time before each activation. The final time is advanced even when no action occurs there. This is how a replay can describe a mismatch closing after the final click. [Replay format](replay-format.md) covers the timeline and diagnostic result.

Board codes, snapshots and replays do not contain a browser storage key, a saved size preference, a per-size best record, a focus location or an animation flag. Those values have different lifetimes and authorities. Tournament JSON carries the event's player names, match codes, turn results and current game state, but it does not carry browser focus or solo preferences. It preserves fractional elapsed time in that current game and its results. Loading a tournament does not change the solo best-score aggregate.

## Browser text is an explicit boundary

The solo reproduction panel is a native `details` element with the summary “Reproduce a game”. It contains the Board code input and Snapshot JSON textarea. Editing either field only edits a draft. `Load board` or `Load snapshot` performs the actual load. The Tournament screen has a separate Tournament JSON textarea with Export tournament and Load tournament actions. Each loader validates its own format before replacing the corresponding state.

